# SemText sem-data-join — one declarative join, four surfaces
# Direction: minimal-tech-light panes on a dark slate page (the reference-site
# render context). The sheet must make the CONTRACT legible, not decorate it:
# a join never renders itself — its bucket surfaces only through a query's
# results table — and failures render one inline alert chip.
#
# MODE 1 — join-success (two named buckets, the join, a query over the joined
#           bucket):
#   visible: two small rendered tables (Users: user-id/name; Orders:
#     order/user-id/total), then the query chrome (caption "QUERY — SELECT …
#     FROM <bucket>", monospace textarea holding the statement, Run button,
#     status "2 rows.") and the results table NAME/TOTAL with two rows
#     (Bao 120, Ada 80). The third user (no orders) has dropped — inner join.
#   must never: render a row for the unmatched user; show a join element
#     itself as a block; disagree with the status row count.
#
# MODE 2 — column-collision (the right side also has a `name` column):
#   visible: same story, results headers USER-ID / NAME / ORDER / ORDERS.NAME
#     / TOTAL — the colliding right column surfaces qualified as orders.name
#     with the product names (Desk lamp, Footrest) beside the person name.
#   must never: print two unqualified NAME columns; keep three rows (u3 drops).
#
# MODE 3 — authoring-error (unknown bucket, key column missing, join name
#           shadowing a source):
#   visible: the source tables render; the join emits one inline accent-red
#     alert chip (.sem-data-error, role=alert) naming the miss in one line;
#     no results table anywhere in the card.
#   must never: render a results table in an error state; show a stack trace;
#     hide or dim the authored sources.
#
# MODE 4 — declaration, JS-off (the bare element, nothing to read):
#   visible: the authored element as plain text (source pane), no chrome,
#     nothing generated, nothing hidden.
#   must never: invent chrome for the invisible declaration.
#
# Reference captures: the fixture renders below plus the site's source-pane
# capture in the parent directory. The authored-row echo line above rendered
# tables is a known in-flight fix (data-payload lane) — draw the tables
# WITHOUT the echo strip.
schema: '0.4'
id: sem-data-join-ux-design
type: image
quality: high
product_targets:
  - semtext.dev
tags:
  - semtext
  - sem-data-join
  - ux-design
  - reference

prompt:
  text: |
    UX design sheet for the SemText `<sem-data-join>` element — a declarative
    inner join that materializes a queryable bucket, rendered in the
    minimal-tech-light theme on a dark slate canvas (bg #0f172a, surface
    #1e293b cards #f8fafc panes, fg #0f172a on panes, muted #64748b, border
    #e2e8f0, accent #7c3aed, error red #dc2626). Four stacked cards, each in a
    rounded 12px light pane with a small mono uppercase caption above it.

    Card 1 — caption "WORKING JOIN (INNER JOIN, QUERY READS THE BUCKET)". Two
    small tables: Users (USER-ID / NAME; u1 Ada, u2 Bao, u3 Cleo) and Orders
    (ORDER / USER-ID / TOTAL; o-101 u2 120, o-102 u1 80). Below them the query
    chrome: a dim caption line "QUERY — SELECT … FROM <bucket> (WHERE / ORDER
    BY / LIMIT)", a white monospace textarea holding
    "SELECT name, total FROM user-orders ORDER BY total DESC", a compact Run
    button, the status "2 rows.", and a results table NAME / TOTAL with Bao
    120 and Ada 80. No row for u3 — the inner join dropped the unmatched user.

    Card 2 — caption "COLLIDING RIGHT COLUMN IS QUALIFIED (ORDERS.NAME)". The
    Orders table here also carries a NAME column (Desk lamp, Footrest — the
    products). The results table reads USER-ID / NAME / ORDER / ORDERS.NAME /
    TOTAL with u2 Bao o-101 Desk lamp 120 and u1 Ada o-102 Footrest 80: both
    name columns visible, the right one qualified, u3 dropped.

    Card 3 — caption "AUTHORING ERROR — ONE INLINE ALERT". The Users table
    renders; beneath it one inline accent-red chip (#dc2626 border, #fef2f2
    fill, dark red text) whose single line reads like
    `sem-data-join: unknown bucket "orders" — buckets: users` with a small
    alert glyph. No results table, no stack trace, no second chip.

    Card 4 — caption "DECLARATION — JS-OFF". A dark source pane with faint
    line numbers showing the bare element
    `<sem-data-join name="user-orders" left="Users" right="Orders"
    on="user-id">` as plain monospace text — no chrome, nothing rendered.

    Typography: clean system sans for captions and status, monospace for
    source, query text and table cells. Generous margins, hairline card
    borders, no decorative noise.

  negative: >
    more or fewer than four cards; a rendered row for the unmatched third user;
    two unqualified NAME columns in the collision results; a results table on
    the error card; a stack trace or raw exception; neon glow, drop shadows or
    gradients on text; light page background; watermark; lorem ipsum; the join
    element drawn as a visible block between the tables.

  style: >
    Flat UI spec sheet, minimal-tech aesthetic, restrained palette, crisp
    12px radii, hairline borders, generous negative space. Reads like a panel
    from a design-system guide describing one component's states.

attachments:
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-data-join/renders/join-collision-scrolled.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-data-join/renders/join-s-primary-big.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-data-join/renders/join-results-primary.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-data-join/renders/join-err-shadowed.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-data-join/sem-data-join-primary-source-1280.png
    role: reference

output:
  dimensions:
    width: 1536
    height: 1024
    aspect_ratio: "3:2"
  formats:
    - format: png
      filename: sem-data-join-ux-design.png

requirements:
  modes:
    - id: join-success
      label: "WORKING JOIN (INNER JOIN, QUERY READS THE BUCKET)"
      must_show:
        - Users and Orders source tables (3 and 2 rows)
        - query textarea with the SELECT statement, Run button, "2 rows." status
        - results table NAME/TOTAL with exactly Bao 120 and Ada 80
      must_never:
        - render a row for u3 (inner join drops the unmatched user)
        - show the join element as a visible block between the tables
    - id: column-collision
      label: "COLLIDING RIGHT COLUMN IS QUALIFIED (ORDERS.NAME)"
      must_show:
        - results headers USER-ID / NAME / ORDER / ORDERS.NAME / TOTAL
        - product names Desk lamp and Footrest under the qualified column
        - exactly two result rows
      must_never:
        - print two unqualified NAME columns
        - keep the unmatched user's row
    - id: authoring-error
      label: "AUTHORING ERROR — ONE INLINE ALERT"
      must_show:
        - the rendered Users table
        - one inline red alert chip naming the miss in a single line
        - no results table
      must_never:
        - render a results table or stack trace
        - show more than one chip
        - hide or dim the authored sources
    - id: js-off
      label: "DECLARATION — JS-OFF"
      must_show:
        - the bare element as plain monospace text in a source pane
      must_never:
        - invent chrome, buttons or tables for the invisible declaration

eval:
  pass_threshold: 0.75
  criteria:
    join-semantics:
      weight: 0.35
      description: >
        The inner-join story is arithmetically true: 2 result rows (Bao 120,
        Ada 80), u3 absent, the colliding column surfaced as ORDERS.NAME.
      fail_signals:
        - a third results row or u3 anywhere in results
        - the collision card showing two unqualified NAME columns
        - status line disagreeing with the results row count
    chrome-fidelity:
      weight: 0.25
      description: >
        Each card carries exactly the chrome its state defines — source
        tables, query caption/textarea/Run/status/results on cards 1-2, one
        alert chip on card 3, bare source text on card 4.
      fail_signals:
        - invented controls beyond Run
        - chrome on the JS-off card
        - missing status line under the query
    error-distinction:
      weight: 0.2
      description: >
        The authoring-error card reads as a single inline red alert, visually
        distinct from tables and from the neutral query status.
      fail_signals:
        - a results table on the error card
        - the alert styled as a table or a modal
        - more than one alert chip
    spec-sheet-quality:
      weight: 0.2
      description: >
        Four labeled cards, consistent monospace, dark page with light panes,
        minimal-tech restraint.
      fail_signals:
        - watermark or lorem ipsum
        - more or fewer than four cards
        - neon or gradient decoration
  reject_if:
    - any card occludes, dims or blurs the authored statement or tables
    - the inner-join results contain a row for the unmatched user
    - the collision results show two unqualified name columns
    - the error card renders a results table or a stack trace
